# Chroma content pack, The Five Callings: THE LAND ROAD (the sixth road, Green's second calling; druid/BRIEF.md, v25
# prep, Packs, draft 10-23), a tendency, never a wall: a [farmer], [forester], [park ranger] or [professional
# beekeeper] (with [gardener or groundskeeper] and [farm hand] on the everyday moments), then [steward of the land] (the
# Known rung, of the community kind: the chair of the town's land trust), then [keeper of the land] (the summit, a facet
# on the steward). Switched off until `callings` is in batch.PACKS; the Packs thread pours it into callings/ as
# callings-land.lib. Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: callings/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md (pack rules, balance
# layout) and the Library's footprints brief (chroma-ideas/v25-prep/library/briefs/FOOTPRINTS.md). Names in title:,
# grants:, takes_if_fails:, holds:, requires: and aims: come from catalogue-land.py, core/*.py and the base catalogue
# (chroma-library/earth_perks_titles.py: farmer, forester, park ranger, professional beekeeper, gardener or
# groundskeeper, farm hand, knowing the woods, the one everyone asks, friend in power, good name in town, apprentice of
# one's own, known across the country); each title takes its tribal and magic form from worlds-land.py (the band's
# keeper of the grove, the great druid of the clans; a druid of the grove, archdruid of the circle).
#
# The land is never a judge (the v25 decision file, section 2): a flood, a drought, a stream running brown or a meadow
# the birds have left is physical, and people read it. No line says the land wants, approves, forgives or punishes, and
# no colour is the land's favourite. Each colour's honest road runs through every moment: White's rules, permits, votes
# and members heard; Blue's survey, count sheets and method; Black's land bought, deals and friends who decide; Red's
# nights on the marsh, campaigns and the rule broken for the land; Green's hands in the soil, the old ways of hedging
# and grazing, and patience. Real and unglamorous where it is: mud, forms, committee evenings, volunteers who do not
# come; a step up is a good year, not a finish line.
#
# Nine moments. Two holder pairs (everyday, holds:, rate .5, gap 1-3, no share: line), whose halves share stages, ages,
# holds:, rate and gap, split the colours 2 and 3 and use the cycle on the pair's header line (s3 and s4: with the
# other roads' pairs, each cycle is used three times in the pack); they take no new title: the next rung is an aims:,
# and grants: gives only a perk plainly earned. Then four life events and the magic world's own event, each even on its
# own (the five one-colour options and the combination half named in its header comment, every colour's mean the same
# inside the moment): the two steps at their real chances (the chair 30, as the brief's senior post in a congregation;
# the summit 6, the same for every colour, with the earned-odds lifts of helps-land.py on top in the engine; no perk on
# failure), the fall, the long shot (3, with grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed) and the circle at the old stones.
# The D halves are the ones the other roads' Earth life events leave unused (D6, both halves; each of D1 to D5 is used
# twice there), and the magic event takes D1b, so the magic world's four pair sets (D1a, D6a, D6b and now D1b) hold
# every pair twice. Every option of a life event leaves one or two footprints: the fields it already has, or a foot:
# just before chance:. Everyday moments carry none (footprints there are deferred to v25.1, tracker F1).
#
# Content limits, never broken: no harm to a child; no suicide or self-harm; nobody is killed. No animal is hurt by any
# act: hedging, grazing, counting and watching are what is done; the shoot and the band's hunts are named and never
# shown. No real body, river, firm, bird reserve, country or year: "the land trust", "the council", "the river
# authority", "the rare birds' body". Modern Earth has no magic and no druid. In the magic world the one supernatural
# act is the old pact of the deep grove's spirits (moment 9): one choice among ordinary ones, priced on a closed:
# approval: line with a backfire, never the person's own gift. Wrongs and closed acts are pickable with plain labels,
# each marked, with a real backfire, spread over the colours: a protected hedge grubbed out (B), a stream check signed
# without looking (W), drains blocked without the tenant's leave (R), a gift's condition kept from the members (G), the
# chair refused on conditions (UR), a spill kept quiet for a friend (WBG), a footpath shut without leave (UBR), the
# spirits' pact (U). Every moment carries earth:, tribal: and magic: lines (moment 9 the magic one alone) and an
# untagged scene for each other world; an option whose act reads differently there carries its own tribal: or magic:
# wording; works and fails lines are written to read in every world.

# ================================================================ holder pairs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- farmer, forester, ranger, beekeeper, groundskeeper or farm hand: calls BG / calls WUR (cycle s3)

== the old hedge along the top lane
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per farmer, forester, park ranger, beekeeper, groundskeeper or farm hand a year: about 1 in 20 are asked to grub out, cut back or lay an old hedge or a line of old trees on the land they work (estimate); old hedges are among the oldest things on farmland, and many were taken out to make fields bigger (estimate); about 1.5 lives in 100 are farmers, 3 in 100 gardeners or groundskeepers and 3 in 100 farm hands (catalogue shares)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: BG
holds: farmer | forester | park ranger | professional beekeeper | gardener or groundskeeper | farm hand
tone: mixed
life: work, nature
horizon: months
roles: boss, mentor
earth: the old hedge along the top lane is overgrown and full of gaps, and the farm wants it gone so the big machines can turn
tribal: the old break of thorn and hazel along the top of the valley has grown wild, and the hunters want it burned off so the herds come through
magic: the old hedge along the top lane of the lord's land has grown wild, and the steward wants it grubbed out so the great ploughs can turn
scene: The hedge along the top lane is older than anything else on the land: hazel, hawthorn and old field maple, laid by hand three generations ago and let go since. It is full of gaps, it leans into the lane, and the contractor's machines cannot turn at the end of the field. {boss} wants it out before the spring, and the contractor can come on Thursday. {mentor} says there are nests in it every year, and that a hedge that old may be protected.
scene W: Old hedges have rules: some may not be taken out without the council's leave, and there is a form for asking. {N} would rather know which kind this is before Thursday.
scene U: Nobody has ever counted what lives in it. {N} suspects the answer is more than anyone thinks: the nests, the bats along it at dusk, the bees in the bank below.
scene B: A field without the hedge is two acres bigger and an hour quicker to work. {N} can see exactly what that is worth, and what the hazel and the timber would fetch.
scene R: {N} has walked that lane since childhood, and the thought of a machine tearing through it makes {N} want to stand in the gateway with arms folded.
scene G: A hedge like that is laid once a generation: each stem cut half through, bent over and woven, and in three springs it is thick again from the root. {N} learned how from {mentor}, and the hands still know it.
scene tribal: Along the top of the valley runs an old break of thorn and hazel, planted by the grandmothers of the band's grandmothers to turn the herds and the wind. It has grown wild and gappy, and {boss}, the leader of the hunters, wants it burned off before the spring so the herds will come through to the hunting ground. {mentor} says the birds have always nested in it.
scene magic: The hedge along the top lane of the lord's land is older than the manor: hazel and thorn and old maple, laid by hand long ago and let go since. {boss}, the lord's steward, wants it grubbed out before the spring so the great ploughs can turn, and the hedge-wardens' roll at the lord's court may have its name on it.
- W | ask the council whether the hedge is protected, and wait for the answer | v: conformity, security | tribal: ask the elders whether the old break may be burned, and wait for their word | magic: ask at the lord's court whether the hedge is on the wardens' roll, and wait | chance: 80
- U | walk the hedge at dawn and dusk for a week, and count what lives in it | v: achievement, universalism | grants: knowing the woods | tribal: watch the old break at dawn and dusk for a moon, and mark what lives in it | chance: 75
- B | grub it out in the winter, before anyone asks whether it is protected | v: power, achievement | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: a protected hedge grubbed out without leave; backfire: the council's officer sees the gap from the lane, and the fine and the order to replant follow | tribal: burn the old break off before the elders can talk about it | magic: grub it out in the winter, before the court's wardens can look at the roll | chance: 85
- R | refuse to take it out, and tell {boss} to find someone else for the job | v: self-direction, universalism | mark: defied an authority | tribal: refuse to burn it, and tell the hunters to find other hands | magic: refuse to grub it out, and tell the steward to find another labourer | chance: 70
- G | lay the hedge by hand this winter, the old way, so it thickens from the root | v: tradition, benevolence | habit | body: light | aims: steward of the land | tribal: cut and bend the old break by hand, the grandmothers' way, so it thickens from the root | magic: lay the hedge by hand this winter, as the hedge-layers of the old manor did | chance: 75
- W>R | apply for a protection order on the hedge, and fight for it at the council | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: ask the elders to forbid any burning on the break, and speak for it at the fire | magic: petition the lord's court to put the hedge on the wardens' roll, and argue for it | chance: 60
- U>G | work out a wider gate for the machines, so the hedge can stay | v: achievement, benevolence | tribal: work out a wider gap for the herds at one end, so the break can stay | magic: work out a wider gateway for the ploughs, so the hedge can stay | chance: 65
- B>W | sell the hazel and the timber, and pay for replanting the gaps by the book | v: power, conformity | aims: steward of the land | tribal: trade the hazel poles at the gathering, and plant new thorn in the gaps as the elders say | magic: sell the hazel and the timber, and replant the gaps as the court's rules ask | chance: 55
- R>U | walk the hedge with the contractor at dawn, and show what lives in it | v: stimulation, universalism | tribal: walk the break with the hunters at dawn, and show them the nests | magic: walk the hedge with the ploughmen at dawn, and show them what lives in it | chance: 70
- G>B | sell the sloes, nuts and poles every autumn, so the hedge pays its way | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: trade the break's sloes and hazel nuts at every autumn gathering, so it earns its keep | magic: sell the sloes, the nuts and the poles at the autumn fair, so the hedge pays its way | chance: 65
works: By the spring it is settled, and the top lane looks the way {N} meant it to.
works: The next summer {N} walks the top lane at dusk, and is glad of what was decided.
fails: The work takes twice as long as it should, through mud and rain, and {boss} is not pleased.
fails: It ends in a quarrel at the gate, and for a season {N} and {boss} barely speak.

== the stream runs brown after the storm
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per farmer, forester, park ranger, beekeeper, groundskeeper or farm hand a year: about 1 in 10 see soil wash off a field into a stream or a ditch after heavy rain (estimate); soil washed off fields is one of the commonest troubles of small rivers on farmland (estimate); about 1.5 lives in 100 are farmers, 3 in 100 gardeners or groundskeepers and 3 in 100 farm hands (catalogue shares)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
calls: WUR
holds: farmer | forester | park ranger | professional beekeeper | gardener or groundskeeper | farm hand
tone: trouble
life: work, nature
horizon: week
roles: colleague, boss
earth: after a night of storm the stream below the fields runs brown with soil, and the pool at the bottom is filling with silt
tribal: after the great storm the stream below the camp runs thick with earth, and the pool where the fish spawn is filling with mud
magic: after the storm the brook below the manor fields runs brown with soil, and the mill pond at the bottom is silting up
scene: After a night of rain like nobody remembers, the stream below the fields runs brown as tea, and the pool at the bottom, where the trout lie in summer, has a bank of silt across it. Up the slope, a field {colleague} ploughed straight down the hill last month has lost its top inch of soil, and more is going every hour. The stream check for the farm's grant is due this week, and {N} is the one who signs it.
scene W: The grant form asks whether the farm's ditches and streams run clean. {N} has always filled it in honestly, and the answer has always been yes.
scene U: Soil goes where water takes it, and water takes the steepest way down. {N} can read on the slope exactly which furrows sent it.
scene B: The washed field up the slope will be worth half what it was by the spring, and {colleague} knows it. A buyer with ready money could do well this week.
scene R: The pool at the bottom was where {N} learned to swim, and now it is mud. {N} is halfway up the hill to {colleague}'s gate before thinking about what to say.
scene G: Grass holds soil, roots hold banks, and willow holds both. {N} looks at the bare bank and thinks of what grew there when the stream ran clear.
scene tribal: After the great storm the stream below the camp runs thick with earth, and the pool where the fish spawn each spring is filling with mud. Up the slope, where {colleague}'s family burned and dug the ground for a root patch this year, the earth is washing away with every shower.
scene magic: After a night of storm the brook below the manor fields runs brown with soil, and the miller's pond at the bottom is silting up. Up the slope, a field {colleague} ploughed straight down the hill has lost its top soil, and the lord's reeve comes this week to ask whether the brook is kept.
- W | sign off the stream check as clean, since it ran clean all summer | v: conformity, security | mark: hid a wrong | self_control: - | closed: approval: a stream check signed without looking; backfire: the inspector walks the stream the next month, the grant is held back, and the farm's name is noted | tribal: tell the elders the pool is as it always was, since it ran clear all summer | magic: tell the reeve the brook is kept, since it ran clear all summer | chance: 80
- U | take jars of water above and below the fields, and find where the soil comes from | v: achievement, universalism | mark: learned a skill | tribal: walk the stream above and below the camp, and find where the earth comes from | chance: 75
- B | offer {colleague} a low price for the washed field, while the mud is fresh | v: power, achievement | tribal: offer {colleague}'s family a bargain for the washed slope, while the mud is fresh | chance: 70
- R | go straight up the hill and tell {colleague} what the ploughing has done | v: self-direction, benevolence | tribal: go straight up the slope and tell {colleague}'s family what their digging has done | chance: 75
- G | drive willow stakes into the bare bank, so roots hold it by the summer | v: tradition, benevolence | body: light | aims: steward of the land | chance: 75
- W>R | ring the river authority, and report the field that washed into the stream | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: take it to the elders at the fire, and name the slope that washed into the stream | magic: report the washed field to the reeve, and name it before the manor court | chance: 60
- U>G | map where grass by the water would catch the soil, and plant those strips | v: achievement, tradition | aims: steward of the land | tribal: mark where grass by the water would catch the earth, and leave those strips unburned | chance: 65
- B>W | apply to the grant scheme for fencing and a grass strip, with every form in order | v: achievement, conformity | tribal: ask the elders for the band's help to fence the stream bank, as the custom allows | magic: petition the lord's steward for fencing and a meadow strip, with every form in order | chance: 70
- R>U | wade in with a rake, and clear the gravel to see how deep the silt lies | v: stimulation, universalism | body: light | tribal: wade in and rake the mud off the spawning stones, to see how deep it lies | magic: wade in with a rake, and clear the millrace gravel to see how deep the silt lies | chance: 65
- G>B | let the wet bottom strip go to rough meadow, and sell the hay | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: let the wet ground by the stream go to tall grass, and trade the cut grass at the gathering | magic: let the wet strip go to meadow, and sell the hay at the manor fair | chance: 65
works: The stream runs clear again by the summer, and the fish are back in the pool at the bottom.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the next big storm sends less soil down the hill.
fails: The next storm sends the soil down again, and the pool at the bottom stays half mud for the year.
fails: It turns into a quarrel up and down the hill, and {colleague} stops waving across the fields.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- steward of the land: calls UG / calls WBR (cycle s4)

== the year the lapwings did not nest
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per steward of the land a year: about 1 in 8 see a bird or a flower the trust's land is known for fail to come back (estimate); birds that nest on the ground in wet meadows have fallen hard on farmland over the last generations (estimate); about 1 life in 1,000 is a steward of the land (catalogue share)
stages: adult mature elder
age: 28-95
calls: UG
holds: steward of the land
tone: trouble
life: community, nature
horizon: months
roles: colleague, mentor
earth: the trust's low meadow has had nesting lapwings every spring for as long as the counts go back, and this spring there are none
tribal: the wet meadow below the grove has had the crested plovers' nests every spring the band remembers, and this spring there are none
magic: the grove's wet meadow has had the crested plovers' nests every spring in the druids' book, and this spring there are none
scene: Every spring for twenty-two years the trust's count sheets have the same line: lapwings nesting on the low meadow, eight pairs, eleven, six, nine. This spring {N} walks the meadow three dawns running and finds none. The ground is drier than it was; dogs run loose from the new path by the estate; the grazier stopped putting cattle on it two years ago. {colleague} from the committee wants to know what the trust means to do.
scene W: The trust's plan for the meadow was written years ago and nobody has looked at it since. There are rules the trust could make, and it has never made them.
scene U: Twenty-two years of count sheets sit in a box in {Ns} back room. Somewhere in them is the year it started to go wrong.
scene B: Nothing happens on the meadow without money: the grazier wants paying, the drains want blocking, the fence wants building. {N} knows who funds this kind of thing.
scene R: {N} lay out on that meadow at dawn as a child and watched the lapwings tumble over it. A spring without that sound is a spring {N} will not accept.
scene G: Lapwings nest in short grass by wet hollows, and cattle make both. The meadow has grown rank and dry since the cattle went, and the birds went with them.
scene tribal: Every spring the band remembers, the crested plovers have tumbled over the wet meadow below the grove and nested in its short grass. This spring {N}, the band's keeper of the grove, walks it three dawns running and finds no nest. The herds have grazed elsewhere since the river moved, and the meadow has gone tall and dry.
scene magic: The druids' book of the grove has a line for every spring: the crested plovers nesting on the wet meadow, eight pairs, eleven, six. This spring {N}, druid of the grove, finds none. A new road runs along the meadow's edge, and the lord's hounds run loose on it.
- W | close the meadow to dogs from spring to midsummer, with a sign on every gate | v: conformity, security | tribal: ask the elders to keep the dogs from the wet meadow until midsummer | magic: ask the lord to keep his hounds off the wet meadow until midsummer | chance: 75
- U | go through twenty years of count sheets, and find the year it began | v: achievement, universalism | grants: the one everyone asks | tribal: ask every old one of the band in turn which spring the plovers began to go | magic: go through every spring in the druids' book, and find the year it began | chance: 80
- B | find the money to pay a grazier to bring cattle back and mow late | v: power, achievement | tribal: trade with the herding band to bring their herds through the wet meadow each spring | magic: strike a bargain with a drover to graze the meadow, paid from the grove's tithes | chance: 70
- R | block the meadow's old drains one night, so the hollows hold water again | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: took a wild risk | closed: approval: drains blocked that also drain the tenant's field; backfire: the tenant's next field floods, and the trust pays for the damage and the apology | tribal: dam the meadow's ditch one night with stones and turf, without asking the elders | magic: block the meadow's drains one night, without the lord's leave | chance: 80
- G | graze the meadow short again with a few old-breed cattle of your own | v: tradition, benevolence | habit | aims: keeper of the land | tribal: bring the band's own small herd to graze the wet meadow each spring | magic: graze the meadow short with a few of the grove's own old-breed cattle | chance: 70
- W>G | put a nesting season into the trust's rules, voted on by the members | v: conformity, tradition | aims: keeper of the land | tribal: ask the elders to make the plovers' season a custom of the band | magic: ask the circle to write a nesting season into the grove's law | chance: 65
- U>W | draw up a meadow plan the grazier and the dog walkers can both keep | v: universalism, conformity | tribal: work out a way for the herds and the dogs that every hearth can keep | magic: draw up a meadow plan the drovers and the lord's huntsmen can both keep | chance: 60
- B>U | interest the university in the meadow, and win a student to study it | v: achievement, power | tribal: interest the wise woman of the next band in the meadow, and have her watch it a season | magic: interest the scholars of the college in the meadow, and win a student to study it | chance: 70
- R>B | rally the volunteers and the local paper, and shame the estate over its dogs | v: stimulation, power | tribal: rally the young hunters to tell every band how the dogs spoiled the meadow | magic: rally the town's broadsheet against the lord's loose hounds | chance: 60
- G>R | lie out on the meadow at dawn each morning, and wait for the first bird back | v: tradition, stimulation | body: light | tribal: lie out on the wet meadow at dawn each morning, watching for the first plover | chance: 70
works: The next spring three pairs are back on the low meadow, tumbling over it at dawn.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the meadow is wetter and quieter by the next spring, and the worry eases.
fails: The next spring is as empty as this one, and {N} is the one who has to say so.
fails: The plan stalls in talk, the summer comes, and the meadow goes on as it was.

== a wood given with strings attached
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
gap: 1-3
times: per steward of the land a year: about 1 in 15 are offered land or a wood as a gift or a bequest with conditions attached (estimate); many land trusts got their first land as a gift or through a will (estimate); about 1 life in 1,000 is a steward of the land (catalogue share)
stages: adult mature elder
age: 28-95
calls: WBR
holds: steward of the land
tone: mixed
life: community, money
horizon: months
roles: elder, colleague
earth: an old landowner offers the trust his wood for nothing, on one condition: that his shooting days go on in it for as long as he lives
tribal: an old hunter of the next band offers the grove's keeper his family's wood on the ridge, on condition that his kin still hunt it each autumn
magic: an old knight with no heir offers the grove his wood on the hill, on condition that his hunting days go on in it as long as he lives
scene: {elder}, who owns the wood above the town, asks {N} round for tea. He is eighty-six and has no children who want the place. He will give the trust the wood, all forty acres of it, for nothing. There is a letter from his lawyer, and one line in it says the shooting days go on, with his friends and the pheasant pens, for as long as he lives. The committee meets on Thursday.
scene W: A gift like this goes before the members, all of it, and the trust's rules say so. A line kept back would be a lie told to the members.
scene U: {N} knows roughly what pheasant pens do to the floor of a wood, but not how much. It could be measured.
scene B: Forty acres for nothing. The trust has never had a chance like it, and {N} knows the old man's lawyer is a friend of a friend.
scene R: Birds raised in pens and driven over guns in the trust's own wood. {N} can already hear what the members will say, and part of {N} agrees with them.
scene G: The wood has stood two hundred years, shoot or no shoot. Old men do not live forever, and woods outlast their owners.
scene tribal: {elder}, an old hunter of the next band, comes to {N}, the band's keeper of the grove, with a gift: his family's wood on the ridge, for the grove to keep. His kin must still hunt there every autumn, as they always have, for as long as he lives.
scene magic: {elder}, an old knight with no heir, sends for {N}, druid of the grove. He will give the grove his wood on the hill, forty acres of old oak, if his hunting days go on in it with his friends for as long as he lives. The circle meets at the new moon.
- W | put the whole letter before the members, the shooting line included | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: tell the whole band at the fire what the gift asks, the hunting included | magic: lay the knight's whole letter before the circle, the hunting clause included | chance: 80
- U | survey what the pheasant pens do to the wood floor, and put a number on it | v: achievement, universalism | tribal: count the deer and the young trees on the ridge, and see what the hunts leave | magic: survey what the knight's hunts do to the floor of the wood, and set it down | chance: 75
- B | bargain with the old man for fewer shooting days and no new pens | v: power, achievement | grants: friend in power | tribal: bargain with the old hunter for fewer hunts and only his own kin | magic: bargain with the knight for fewer hunting days and no new kennels | chance: 70
- R | refuse the wood outright while the shooting goes on in it | v: self-direction, universalism | mark: made an enemy | tribal: refuse the ridge wood outright while another band hunts in it | magic: refuse the knight's wood outright while his hunts go on in it | chance: 75
- G | take the wood, keep the shoot, and leave the shooting line out of the minutes | v: tradition, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a gift's condition kept from the members; backfire: the members find the shooting line in the deeds at the next meeting, and two of the committee resign | tribal: take the wood, and say nothing at the fire of the old hunter's terms | magic: take the wood, and leave the hunting clause out of the circle's record | chance: 85
- W>G | take the gift only if the deed keeps the wood standing for good | v: conformity, tradition | tribal: take the gift only if the old hunter swears at the fire that the wood will never be cut | magic: take the gift only if the deed binds every heir to keep the wood standing | chance: 60
- U>W | write rules for the shoot: no new pens, and no shooting in the nesting months | v: universalism, conformity | aims: keeper of the land | tribal: work out rules for the autumn hunts: no fires, and no hunting in the birthing season | magic: write rules for the hunts: no new kennels, and no hunting in the nesting months | chance: 65
- B>U | have a lawyer friend read the deed, and find what else it binds | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask a trader who knows the ways of gifts what else the old hunter's terms bind | magic: have a clerk of the court read the deed, and find what else it binds | chance: 70
- R>B | take the whole thing to the town's paper, and let the town decide | v: stimulation, power | tribal: tell it at every fire in the valley, and let the bands decide | magic: take it to the town's broadsheet, and let the town decide | chance: 65
- G>R | walk the wood with the old man, and hear why the shoot matters to him | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: made a friend | tribal: walk the ridge wood with the old hunter, and hear why the hunts matter to him | magic: walk the wood with the old knight, and hear why the hunts matter to him | chance: 55
works: By the spring it is settled, and {N} can face the members at the meeting with a clear head.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the wood above the town is still standing, and the trust's name is still good.
fails: The wood splits everyone down the middle, and two old friends stop speaking.
fails: It drags on all winter, nobody is pleased with the end of it, and {N} is the one they blame.

# ================================================================ steps

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. the land trust needs a chair (pairs D6a)
# The Known rung: the old chair of the town's land trust steps down, and the members look for a farmer, forester,
# ranger or beekeeper of three years or more to keep its meadow, wood and river (tenure, and LIFE_LAND in
# engine-conditions-land.py; with the land on, the Packs thread adds the ten third-step titles of LAND_WAYS_LAND to
# holds:). Every colour's way of taking it on is a try at the chair's real chance (30, the brief's senior post in a
# congregation): `title: steward of the land`. White's formal nomination and Green's standing rest on [good name in
# town]; someone without it is chosen only by the members' leave (`requires: good name in town | without: approval |
# lacking: .5`). The pairs at 82 are the honest other ways: a year beside the old chair first, the trust's count taken
# on instead, the chair refused until the council hands over the low meadow (a closed: approval act), the deputy put
# forward, and the members asked who they want. Every colour's mean is 56.

== the land trust needs a chair
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 28-90
holds: farmer | forester | park ranger | professional beekeeper
tenure: 3-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .03 .04 .03
drivers: community+.3 prosper+.1
times: per farmer, forester, park ranger or beekeeper of three years or more a year: about 1 in 30 are asked to stand for the chair of the town's land, wildlife or commons trust (estimate); about one such trust a town of 10,000 to 20,000, its chair held about ten years (the land trusts' census and the wildlife and commons groups; estimate); about 1 life in 1,000 is a steward of the land (catalogue share)
likelier: a chair stepping down, a fight over a field everyone knows, years of work parties, good years with money for land
rarer: a chair who stays twenty years, a trust that has folded, a town that has never had one
gap: 2-5
domain: community.5
tone: mixed
life: community, nature
horizon: months
roles: mentor, colleague, rival, boss
earth: the old chair of the town's land trust steps down, and the members ask who will keep the meadow, the wood and the river after her
tribal: the old keeper of the grove can no longer walk the hunting grounds, and the bands ask who will keep the grove and the grounds after her
magic: the old druid of the grove goes to her rest, and the circle asks who will keep the grove after her
scene: {mentor} has chaired the town's land trust for nineteen years: the low meadow, the wood above the town, two miles of river bank and a committee of nine who argue about all of them. At the yearly meeting she stands up and says this is her last year. Afterwards three members come and find {N} by the tea urn. {rival}, who sits on the council's planning committee, has already said he would do it.
scene W: The trust has a constitution, and the chair is elected under it by the members, with a seconder and a vote. {N} has read it twice.
scene U: {N} has opinions about the trust: the counts nobody looks at again, the plan nobody follows. In the chair, they could be put right.
scene B: The chair is where the money, the council and the land deals meet. {N} would rather hold it than watch {rival} hold it.
scene R: {N} has spent more Saturdays in the trust's mud than anyone. If anyone is going to fight for that meadow, it should be {N}.
scene G: {N} has worked this valley's land for years, and knows which field floods first and where the orchids come up. Nobody knows it better, in all its seasons.
scene tribal: The old keeper of the grove, {mentor}, has grown too stiff to walk the hunting grounds and says so at the fire. The bands look round for who will keep the grove, the burning and the grounds after her, and {rival} of the other hearth has already said he will.
scene magic: The old druid of the grove, {mentor}, has gone to her rest after forty years, and the circle of the grove will name her successor at the next new moon. {rival}, who has the ear of the lord's steward, has already put his name forward.
