- WR | ask the members at the yearly meeting who they want, and abide by it | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: ask every hearth at the fire who they want to keep the grove | magic: ask every druid of the circle who they want, and abide by it | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 82
works: Within the season it is settled, and the valley turns to {N} first.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the meadow, the wood and the river are in good hands, and {N} sleeps well that month.
fails: The choice falls on {rival}, and {N} has to smile through it with everyone else.
fails: It goes badly at the one meeting that mattered, and the choice falls on someone from outside the valley.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6. the country comes to see the land (pairs D6b)
# The summit, a facet on the steward held for five years or more (LIFE_LAND): the land the trust has kept is suddenly
# known, and people come from across the country to see it. Every colour's way of taking the name on is a try at the
# summit's real chance (6): `title: keeper of the land` with `grants: known across the country`. A step at real odds,
# not a long shot, so no perk on failure, and no mark on the singles (the title and the name are their two footprints).
# The pairs at 80 are the honest other ways: a café that pays two wardens, the far groups visited on their own land, two
# young wardens taught, a reserve made by law, and a careful letter to each town that asks. Every colour's mean is 43.

== the country comes to see the land
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-100
holds: steward of the land
tenure: 5-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .01 .02 .02
drivers: era+.3 fortune+.2
times: per steward of the land of five years or more a year: about 1 in 50 find people coming from across the country to see the land the trust keeps, after a story, a programme or a rare bird (estimate); a few hundred living people in a large country are known for a reserve, a wood or a river they kept or brought back (estimate); about 3 lives in 100,000 become keepers of the land (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: years of the same valley's counts, a rare bird or flower back, a programme on the radio or the television, a mentor who sends visitors on
rarer: a trust with nothing to show yet, a fight with the council, a move away
gap: 5-15
domain: community.5
tone: joy
life: community, nature, publiclife
horizon: years
roles: boss, colleague, mentor, friend
earth: the meadow, the wood and the river the trust has kept for years are suddenly famous, and coaches of visitors come from across the country
tribal: runners from bands far beyond the valley come to see the grove and the grounds the keeper has kept, and to learn how
magic: druids and scholars from distant groves of the realm come to walk the grove's woods, and ask its druid how it was done
scene: It began with a paragraph in a big paper about the orchids on the low meadow, then a radio piece, then a programme crew in waders in the river. Now coaches come on Sundays, the car park is full by ten, schools write every week, and a minister's office has asked whether the trust's way could be copied across the country. {boss}, the leader of the council, wants to know what {N} means to do about it.
scene W: People come because the land is kept well, and it is kept well because the rules are kept. {N} would like to keep it that way, however many come.
scene U: Twenty years of counts, and now there are enough eyes on the land to learn what really worked. {N} has a question in mind that nobody has asked.
scene B: A name like this opens doors: the council's land committee, the national bodies, the money that decides where land goes. {N} knows it will not last forever.
scene R: The coaches, the cameras, the children running down the boardwalk. {N} feels every bit of it, and wants every one of them to love the place.
scene G: {N} never set out to be known. The meadow came back the way meadows do, one season on another, and now people come from far away to stand in it.
scene tribal: Runners have come from bands three valleys away to see the grove and the grounds {N} has kept, and two elders have walked for days to sit at {Ns} fire and learn how the herds came back. The traders carry the story from camp to camp, and {boss}, the eldest of the band, asks what {N} means to do about it.
scene magic: Druids and scholars from distant groves of the realm have come to walk the grove's woods, and a royal clerk writes that the crown would have every grove kept the same way. {boss}, the lord's steward, wants to know what {N}, druid of the grove, means to do.
