- BR | open a café by the car park, and let its takings pay two wardens | v: achievement, stimulation | tribal: trade with the visiting bands for food and furs, to keep two young watchers at the grounds | magic: let an innkeeper open by the grove's gate, and let the rent pay two wardens | foot: means money+.05 | chance: 80
- RG | go out to the far groups yourself, and walk their own land with them | v: benevolence, stimulation | body: light | tribal: walk out to the far bands yourself, and walk their own grounds with them | magic: ride out to the far groves yourself, and walk their own woods with them | foot: means time-.06 | chance: 80
- UB | train two young wardens to take over one day, and teach them the whole work | v: achievement, benevolence | grants: apprentice of one's own | binds | tribal: take two young ones of the band beside you, and teach them all you know of the grove | magic: take two young druids into the grove, and teach them all you know of its woods | chance: 80
- WG | ask the council to make the meadow and the wood a reserve by law | v: conformity, tradition | tribal: ask the gathering of the bands to swear the grove and the grounds be kept for good | magic: ask the crown to make the grove's woods a reserve by royal charter | foot: place rule.counsel | chance: 80
- WU | write each town that asks a careful letter on keeping its own land | v: universalism, benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: send each far band's runner home with a careful word on keeping their own grounds | magic: write each grove that asks a careful letter on keeping its own woods | chance: 80
works: By the end of the year {Ns} name is known far beyond the valley, and the visitors keep coming.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the land is well kept and well loved, and the valley's own still come first.
fails: The fuss dies down after a season, and the valley is quiet again.
fails: The talk moves on to another place, and {N} goes back to the ordinary work of the land, a little relieved.

# ================================================================ fall and wrong

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. what runs into the river at night (triads D6a)
# A friend's farm is letting slurry into the stream at night, and the steward, a farmer or a ranger walking the bank
# finds the pipe. Nothing in it judges anyone: the water is cloudy, the pool below is empty of fish, and the stream
# will mend once the pipe stops. Tell or keep quiet. The wrong sits on the WBG triad (White's loyalty to a
# friend over the rule, Black's farm saved from the fine, Green's trust that the stream will clear by itself): say
# nothing (hid a wrong; closed: approval: a spill seen and kept quiet; when it fails the town hears it, and
# `takes_if_fails: good name in town`, the backfire). The others: the river authority rung that night, samples taken, one
# week given to mend it, the paper told, the pipe blocked together and reported in the morning, the samples laid before
# the town meeting, the friend stood by through the fine, a weekly walk of the river with the friend, and a grant found
# for a covered store. Chances are set by act and the singles solved around the triads until every colour's mean is 70.

== what runs into the river at night
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-95
holds: steward of the land | keeper of the land | farmer | forester | park ranger | professional beekeeper
per_year: 0 0 .01 .015 .015 .01
drivers: trouble+.2 prosper+.1
times: per steward of the land, farmer, forester, ranger or beekeeper a year: about 1 in 70 find a neighbour's farm or works letting something into a stream at night (estimate); slurry and silage leaks are among the commonest causes of fish lost from small rivers on farmland (water regulators' reports; estimate); about 1.5 lives in 100 are farmers (catalogue share)
likelier: a wet winter and a full slurry store, an old pipe, a farm in debt, a dry summer with low water
rarer: a new store with a cover, a river warden who walks the bank, a farm with time to mend things
gap: 5-15
domain: community.5
tone: trouble
life: community, nature, friends
horizon: week
roles: friend, colleague, boss
earth: walking the river at night, the steward finds a pipe from a friend's dairy running slurry into the stream
tribal: walking the river at dusk, the keeper of the grove finds a friend's family soaking hides in the pool above the band's drinking water
magic: walking the river at night, the druid finds a pipe from a friend's dye-works running into the stream above the town
scene: Walking the river bank at night after the first warm rain, {N} smells it before seeing it: a pipe from the slurry store at {friend}'s dairy, half hidden in the nettles, running dark into the stream. Below it the water is cloudy for a hundred yards, and the pool by the bridge is empty of fish. {friend} has had a bad year with the milk price and has a letter from the bank on the kitchen table. The river authority has a line that answers at any hour.
scene W: The law on this is plain, and so is the duty of anyone who keeps land: report it, the same night, whoever it is.
scene U: How much, for how long, and from where exactly? Without samples taken tonight, nobody will ever know for sure.
scene B: {friend} owes the bank, and a fine would finish the farm. {N} could use that, or protect it.
scene R: The pool by the bridge was full of trout last week. {N} wants to shout, and is frightened for {friend} at the same time.
scene G: A stream mends once what fouls it stops: the water runs on, and the fish come back from upstream within a few seasons. What matters is that the pipe stops tonight.
scene tribal: Walking the river at dusk, {N}, the band's keeper of the grove, finds {friend}'s family soaking a winter's hides in the still pool above the place where the band draws its water. The water below runs cloudy and sour, and the little fish have gone from the shallows. {friend}'s family had a hard winter, and the hides are their trade at the gathering.
scene magic: Walking the river at night, {N}, druid of the grove, finds a pipe from {friend}'s dye-works running into the stream above the town, the water below it stained and sour. {friend} owes the moneylenders, and the lord's water bailiff would close the works for good.
