# ================================================================ long shot

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8. the cranes nest on the old marsh (triads D6b)
# A pair of cranes, gone from the country's marshes for generations, nests on the old marsh the trust let flood ten
# years ago. It comes to a steward of two years or more, or a farmer, forester, ranger or beekeeper of eight, from
# thirty-five, the summit's first age (`tenure: 2-99` on the holds; LIFE_LAND_LONGSHOTS has the exact years). The long
# shot is the summit at 3 in every colour, `title: keeper of the land; steward of the land` (the facet first, so a
# farmer who meets it skips one rung, as the pack's rule allows), with `grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed`
# (core/longshot.py), so every single fits everyone who meets the moment. The birds come because water came back, not
# as a reward for anyone. The triads at 84, the ways to weigh it: the nest guarded by a rota, the trust's newsletter, a
# hide at the far edge, the footpath shut without leave (a closed: law act), or the nest kept a secret. Every colour's
# mean is 43.5.

== the cranes nest on the old marsh
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-95
holds: steward of the land | farmer | forester | park ranger | professional beekeeper
tenure: 2-99
per_year: 0 0 0 .01 .01 .01
drivers: era+.2 fortune+.3
times: per steward of the land of two years or more, or farmer, forester, ranger or beekeeper of eight, a year: about 1 in 100 see a rare bird or plant come back to land they keep (estimate); cranes and other great marsh birds have come back to nest in a few places after centuries away, and each return draws the country's eyes (estimate); about 3 lives in 100,000 become keepers of the land (catalogue share)
likelier: a marsh let flood years before, a dry spring elsewhere, a quiet valley, a year when the papers want good news
rarer: drained land, a busy path, a hard winter
gap: 6-15
domain: community.5
tone: joy
life: community, nature
horizon: months
roles: mentor, colleague, rival, boss
earth: a pair of cranes, gone from the country's marshes for generations, nests on the old marsh the trust let flood, and the country's eyes turn to the valley
tribal: a pair of great cranes, a bird the band knows only from the oldest songs, nests on the old marsh below the grove, and the bands come to look
magic: a pair of great cranes, a bird the realm's old books call lost, nests on the grove's old marsh, and the realm's eyes turn to the valley
scene: Ten years ago {N} and a dozen volunteers blocked the ditches on the old marsh and let it flood. This April, at dawn, {N} hears a sound like a trumpet from the reeds and, through the glasses, sees two tall grey birds: a pair of cranes, gone from the country's marshes since anyone's great-grandparents were young. By May there is a nest. Nobody else knows yet, and {mentor} says that if word gets out, half the country will be in the lane by the weekend.
scene W: There are laws for a rare bird's nest, and a body that keeps the list. {N} has the form and the number, and a careful habit of doing things properly.
scene U: Nobody in the country has watched cranes nest for generations. Every day of what happens on that marsh is something no one alive has seen.
scene B: A bird like this could make the trust's name, and {Ns} with it: the money, the programme, the minister on the boardwalk. {N} knows how quickly a chance like this goes.
scene R: {N} cannot stop grinning. Ten years of mud and arguments, and now this, at dawn, in the reeds.
scene G: Water came back to the marsh, then the reeds, the frogs and the herons, and now the cranes. It took ten years, the way such things take, and nothing could have hurried it.
scene tribal: Ten winters ago {N}, the band's keeper of the grove, had the young ones dam the ditches on the old marsh below the grove and let it flood. Now, at dawn, a call like a horn comes from the reeds, and two tall grey birds stand in the water: great cranes, a bird the band knows only from the oldest songs. Soon there is a nest, and nobody else has seen it yet.
scene magic: Ten winters ago {N}, druid of the grove, let the old marsh flood again. Now, at dawn, a call like a horn comes from the reeds: a pair of great cranes, a bird the realm's old books call lost, and soon a nest. Nobody else knows, and {mentor} says that if word reaches the capital, half the court will ride out to see.
